-- | Feed-id format predicate. Deliberately dependency-free (DA.Text only).
--
-- Why this lives here and not only in oracle-chainlink: that package decodes a Chainlink report and
-- therefore depends on the Chainlink `domain` DAR. Importing `isV3FeedId` from there would drag the
-- whole Chainlink dependency closure into the price store, and hence onto the participant of every
-- party that is merely an informee on a transaction that reads a price. `RegisterFeed` and
-- `RecordVerifiedPrice` need this predicate; nothing else about Chainlink belongs on this side of
-- the package boundary.
module Palladium.Oracle.PriceStore.FeedId where

import DA.Text qualified as T

-- | Check if a feed ID is a V3 feed (starts with "0003").
--
-- Two callers, for two different reasons:
--   * the price-write path asserts it before the report's fields are read by position —
--     a different schema version would otherwise be silently misread, taking the price from the
--     wrong word. That check lives in the Chainlink feeder package, where the decoding happens, and
--     `RecordVerifiedPrice` repeats it on this side of the boundary.
--   * `RegisterFeed` asserts it on the raw id so a label can never be bound to an id that no
--     verified report could ever carry — which would leave that label permanently unpriceable with
--     no error until someone tried to use it.
--
-- Note the coupling this creates: the store is otherwise feeder-agnostic, but this one predicate
-- hard-codes the Chainlink Data Streams V3 convention. That is correct while Chainlink is the only
-- price source. Adding a second source means relaxing this, not working around it.
isV3FeedId : Text -> Bool
isV3FeedId = (== "0003") . T.take 4
